Z||000000||Scott_M||06-26-2000||02:18 AM||||Can anyone tell me what the average price to expect for a mechanic to bleed my brakes? In replacing the brake pads on my 87 SE, I was forced to take off the rear calipers in order to get the piston pushed in. I bled them myself, but they still feel spongy so I thought I'd take it in.  I also was thinking of getting the AC system drained because that R-134 conversion looks good to me right now, and I was also wondering how much that should cost. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||-=shame=-||06-26-2000||08:30 AM||||As far as the A/C can't tell ya, I lucked out and work across from a HVAC shop. (Great Guys) Now as far as the brakes really just bleed them again. The air can sometimes work around in the lines. Especially In those high spots, just because air is put under pressure doesn't mean it will go down hill. Hope that helps.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||06-26-2000||08:35 PM||||I paid about $125-150 for the 134a A/C conversion including a rebuilt compressor.<P>I had gotten that quote from on A/C shop prior to having the system get recharged.  I got a $450 quote from another shop in Florida while on a road trip to fix the system.<P>When my A/C died the car was at Twin Lakes Fiero and FieroBob fixed it.||||reg||1
